{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And He appeared.</b> R. Ḥama bar Ḥanina said: it was the third day after his circumcision, and the Holy One, blessed be He, came and visited him. And this is also the implication — in no place do we find the expression 'and He appeared' without speech or talk written after it, but here it is not written; and why did He not appear except to visit him? And one may say: the main reason the Holy One, blessed be He, appeared to Abraham was only for the matter of Sodom, but the matter of the angels interrupted the account, and also one of them came for the purpose of Sodom. Another interpretation: 'and He appeared to him' — as he goes on to explain, that three angels came to him; in many places an angel is called by the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, as though he himself were speaking, as it is written: 'for My name is within him,' and permission was given him to speak in the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written of Moses: 'and an angel of HaShem appeared to him,' etc., and 'HaShem saw that he turned aside to see,' and likewise at the Binding." "<b>In the oaks of Mamre.</b> Rashi explained: he gave him counsel concerning circumcision, as is found in Bereshit Rabbah. At the time the Holy One, blessed be He, told him to circumcise himself, he went and consulted three of his friends. Aner said to him: 'You are already ninety-nine years old and you go and afflict yourself?' He said to him: 'Eshkol — why do you go and afflict yourself among your enemies?' He said to him: 'Mamre — your El stood by you in the fiery furnace, in the famine, in the kings, and this thing that He told you to do — to circumcise yourself — will you not listen to Him?' etc. And if you ask: how is it possible to say of a great and holy man, beloved of the Place, like Abraham, who stood through ten trials, that he would take counsel whether to fulfill circumcision, a commandment of the Holy One, blessed be He, or not? Rather, we must say: Abraham certainly did not suspend his own judgment to follow the opinion of others in doing his Creator's will, but in his mind he wished to weigh the opinions of Aner, Eshkol, and Mamre, and he found uprightness in none of them except Mamre. And one may say: Aner, Eshkol, and Mamre too were circumcised, as is found in the Aggadah: Aner, Eshkol, and Mamre all died on account of the matter of circumcision. And one may say: he gave him counsel about a plant to heal the wound. And he sat at the entrance of the tent in the fifth hour, which is the mealtime of Torah scholars. And that righteous man stood at the entrance of his tent to see if any passerby might pass by, to bring him into his house." "<b>Like the heat of the day.</b> For the honor of Abraham this verse was written, meaning: at the height of the day's heat. And it is not the way of guests to lodge overnight but only to eat and move on; therefore he said to them, 'afterwards you shall pass on.' But Lot — the reason he said to them 'and lodge' is because 'the two angels came to Sodom in the evening.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The text '(לא)' appears in parentheses in the source; translated as 'not' in its natural position."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:43.066571454Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}